<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Essenes</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Essenes were a Jewish religious movement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> period, active from about the 2nd century BCE to the 1st century CE. Described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, Philo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a>, they lived communally, shared their property and practised strict ritual purity. Many scholars link them to the community at Qumran that produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea-scrolls">Dead Sea Scrolls</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/essenes.jpg" alt="Essenes" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wilson44691 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:QumranLivingQuarters.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Essene movement likely arose as a distinct group among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in the time of Jonathan Apphus, driven by disputes over Jewish law and the belief that Jonathan's high priesthood was illegitimate. Most scholars think the Essenes seceded from the Zadokite priests. They attributed their interpretation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> to an early leader, the Teacher of Righteousness, possibly a legitimate high priest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> names them as one of the three sects of Jewish philosophy, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharisees">Pharisees</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadducees">Sadducees</a>. Josephus and Philo give their number as about four thousand, living in many towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> places them above Ein Gedi on the west side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea">Dead Sea</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> and Philo describe a strictly communal life, often compared to later Christian monasticism. Property was held in common, a leader was elected and obeyed, and the Essenes owned no slaves and did not trade. They were forbidden to swear oaths or sacrifice animals, and carried weapons only against robbers. Many groups were celibate, though Josephus mentions another order that married after a three-year engagement. After a three-year probation a new member swore to practise piety and righteousness and to preserve the books of the Essenes and the names of the angels. They believed in the immortality of the soul, ate communal meals and practised ritual immersion in water.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea-scrolls">Dead Sea Scrolls</a>, found at Qumran on the northwestern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea">Dead Sea</a>, are commonly believed to be the Essenes' library, and Qumran is believed to have been home to an Essene community. The scrolls include parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> and sectarian works such as the Community Rule, the Damascus Document and the War Scroll. The link is disputed: Norman Golb argues that the ruins were a fortress, and Lawrence Schiffman calls the community Sadducean. The conventional view is that the Essenes disappeared after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-jewish-roman-war">First Jewish–Roman War</a>, which destroyed Qumran, though no direct source supports this. Some scholars see their influence on Mandaeism and early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Essenes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the following centuries, under Persian, Greek and then Roman rule, the scriptures were gathered and edited and the religion of the Second Temple period developed, with its priests, its scribes and its rival schools: the Pharisees, the Sadducees, the <b>Essenes</b> and others.</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…of Roland de Vaux and Józef Milik, almost universally held until the 1990s, points to likenesses between the Community Rule and Josephus's account of the <b>Essenes</b>, inkwells in a room de Vaux called the scriptorium, ritual baths, and Pliny the Elder's report of Essenes near the Dead Sea.</blockquote><a href="/work/dead-sea-scrolls">Dead Sea Scrolls · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>André Dupont-Sommer proposed that Ashoka's missions influenced the <b>Essenes</b> and Therapeutae.</blockquote><a href="/work/edicts-of-ashoka">Edicts of Ashoka · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Pharisees, Sadducees and <b>Essenes</b> emerged in this era.</blockquote><a href="/state/hasmonean-kingdom">Hasmonean Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
